From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 11:19:39 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: sep: power the fingerprint sensor off for
 system sleep

The sensor driver had no system sleep callbacks. A sensor left powered
by the attach-time bring-up stayed powered through every s2idle and deep
sleep, and a capture work item that outlived the suspend drain could
still reach the bus and the power rails while devices went down.

macOS 27.2 (25G83) does this around a sleep, read statically from
AppleMesaSEPDriver, AppleBiometricSensor and IOBiometricFamily:
AppleMesaSEPDriver has two power states, and its on state needs its
parent's power, so a system sleep calls setPowerState(0). That starts
a zero-delay timer and returns a 20 ms acknowledgment bound; the
handler cancels the driver's timers and pending command, then
sensorDisable(true, userOverride = false) has AppleMesa wait for its state
machine, mask the data-ready event source, send 80 31 00 07 00 00 00
(opcode 0x31, framed like the idle command 0x30) transmit-only, and
cut the power only when the user turned the sensor off. The generic
transfer endpoint to the enclave is then disabled on the AP side, with
no message to the enclave, and the power change is acknowledged. On
wake it unmasks the event source, reads the 16-byte status and runs a
power cycle and patch reload if the status is bad or unreadable, then
restarts whatever the current biometric command needs.

Here the sleep command goes out and the power is then cut, whatever the
reason for the sleep. Every Linux bring-up starts with a power cycle and
registers the sensor with the enclave again, so a sensor powered off at
suspend is powered on demand by the next enrol or verify, which is what
the wake-time recovery on macOS amounts to. The resume only lets
requests through again. Between the two, bus and power requests fail
with -ESHUTDOWN. A suspend that finds the sensor already off, which it
is after any enrol or verify, touches no hardware, and the power-off
skips the 10 ms settling delay, which the next power cycle has anyway.
A power-off that fails is logged and does not fail the sleep.

Nothing else needs to change for the sleep. The PM notifier already ends
any capture and drains the work before devices suspend. The enclave keeps
its state across the sleep, and the next verify clears and registers
the sensor again as every verify does. spi-apple programs the mode, chip
select timing, clock divider, word size, counts and interrupts for each
message or transfer, because runtime PM gates its power domain between
messages, so a controller that lost its state in a deep sleep is already
handled. The AP GPIO restore after a deep sleep puts back the data-ready
pin's trigger mode. The callbacks belong to the sensor's SPI driver, so a
machine without the sensor runs none of this.

KUnit covers the sleep sequence and the power tracking with faked
hardware. They pass, built with the shim's own code on the host with
-Wall -Wextra -Werror, and six mutations of the sequence each fail at
least one case.

Signed-off-by: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig            |   5 +-
 drivers/soc/apple/sensor_shim.c      | 152 ++++++++++++++++++------
 drivers/soc/apple/sensor_shim_test.c | 169 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 286 insertions(+), 40 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 drivers/soc/apple/sensor_shim_test.c

diff --git a/drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig b/drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig
index 9001a709fb..fca5aefea8 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig
@@ -214,8 +214,9 @@ config APPLE_SEP_KUNIT_TEST
 	  mailbox message encoding and decoding, the shared-memory registration
 	  limits, the rules that keep a keybag's lookup identity apart from
 	  the identity the enclave reports, the format and validation of the
-	  Linux host-state store, and the xART guard's decisions and its list
-	  of Linux's records. The tests touch no hardware.
+	  Linux host-state store, the xART guard's decisions and its list
+	  of Linux's records, and how the fingerprint sensor is put to sleep.
+	  The tests touch no hardware.
 
 	  If unsure, say N.
 
diff --git a/drivers/soc/apple/sensor_shim.c b/drivers/soc/apple/sensor_shim.c
index bed228b4d1..5536de938e 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/apple/sensor_shim.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/apple/sensor_shim.c
@@ -15,6 +15,7 @@
 #include <linux/of_address.h>
 #include <linux/of_platform.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/pm.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
 #include <linux/spi/spi.h>
 #include <linux/unaligned.h>
@@ -40,8 +41,21 @@ MODULE_PARM_DESC(sensor_spi_mode,
 struct sep_sensor_lifetime {
 	/* Serializes sensor operations with this device's resource teardown. */
 	struct mutex lock;
+	/* The power last written; a failed write counts as on. */
+	bool powered;
+	/* Set across a system sleep: bus and power requests are refused. */
+	bool asleep;
 };
 
+/* The power line or rails and the bus, with no settling delays. */
+struct sep_sensor_hw {
+	int (*set_power)(int on);
+	int (*send)(const void *tx, size_t len);
+};
+
+/* Puts the sensor in its low-power state; macOS sends it before a sleep. */
+static const u8 sep_sensor_cmd_sleep[] = { 0x80, 0x31, 0x00, 0x07, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00 };
+
 static DEFINE_MUTEX(sep_sensor_registry_lock);
 static struct sep_sensor_lifetime *sep_active_sensor;
 
@@ -58,6 +72,7 @@ static struct sep_sensor_lifetime *sep_lock_sensor(void)
 }
 
 static void sep_sensor_irq_teardown_locked(void);
+static int sep_sensor_xfer_tx_locked(const void *tx, size_t len);
 
 static struct spi_device *sep_spi;
 static struct gpio_desc *sep_power;
@@ -328,6 +343,52 @@ static void sep_release_power(void)
 	sep_power_source = SEP_POWER_NONE;
 }
 
+static int sep_sensor_set_power_now(int on)
+{
+	if (sep_power_source == SEP_POWER_SMC_RAILS)
+		return sep_smc_set_power(on);
+	if (!sep_power)
+		return -ENODEV;
+	gpiod_set_value_cansleep(sep_power, on);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static const struct sep_sensor_hw sep_hw = {
+	.set_power = sep_sensor_set_power_now,
+	.send = sep_sensor_xfer_tx_locked,
+};
+
+static int sep_sensor_write_power(struct sep_sensor_lifetime *sensor,
+				  const struct sep_sensor_hw *hw, int on)
+{
+	int rc = hw->set_power(on);
+
+	/* A failed write may have taken effect. */
+	sensor->powered = rc || on;
+	return rc;
+}
+
+/*
+ * As macOS does for a sleep: the sleep command, then the power off. The next
+ * bring-up's power cycle powers the sensor again, so a resume does not.
+ * Returns the power-off's error.
+ */
+static int sep_sensor_sleep_locked(struct sep_sensor_lifetime *sensor,
+				   const struct sep_sensor_hw *hw)
+{
+	WRITE_ONCE(sensor->asleep, true);
+	if (!sensor->powered)
+		return 0;
+	/* Best effort: only the power-off matters. */
+	hw->send(sep_sensor_cmd_sleep, sizeof(sep_sensor_cmd_sleep));
+	return sep_sensor_write_power(sensor, hw, 0);
+}
+
+static int sep_sensor_awake_locked(struct sep_sensor_lifetime *sensor)
+{
+	return READ_ONCE(sensor->asleep) ? -ESHUTDOWN : 0;
+}
+
 #define SEP_CS_TIMING_SOFTWARE	0
 #define SEP_CS_TIMING_HOOK_BYPASSED	1
 #define SEP_CS_TIMING_HARDWARE	2
@@ -462,6 +523,35 @@ static void sep_sensor_remove(struct spi_device *spi)
 	mutex_unlock(&sep_sensor_registry_lock);
 }
 
+static int sep_sensor_suspend(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct sep_sensor_lifetime *sensor = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	bool was_on;
+	int rc;
+
+	mutex_lock(&sensor->lock);
+	was_on = sensor->powered;
+	rc = sep_sensor_sleep_locked(sensor, &sep_hw);
+	mutex_unlock(&sensor->lock);
+	/* Failing the sleep would keep the machine awake; the next bring-up recovers. */
+	if (rc)
+		dev_warn(dev, "sep sensor: power-off for sleep failed (%d)\n", rc);
+	else if (was_on)
+		dev_dbg(dev, "sep sensor: off for sleep\n");
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/* No lock: a capture still waiting on the data-ready line may hold it. */
+static int sep_sensor_resume(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct sep_sensor_lifetime *sensor = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+
+	WRITE_ONCE(sensor->asleep, false);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static DEFINE_SIMPLE_DEV_PM_OPS(sep_sensor_pm_ops, sep_sensor_suspend, sep_sensor_resume);
+
 /*
  * Its own compatible, deliberately not spidev's: capture must stay in the
  * kernel, and a spidev node would leak an image to userspace. No
@@ -485,6 +575,7 @@ static struct spi_driver sep_sensor_driver = {
 	.driver = {
 		.name = "apple-mesa",
 		.of_match_table = sep_sensor_of_match,
+		.pm = pm_sleep_ptr(&sep_sensor_pm_ops),
 	},
 	.id_table = sep_sensor_spi_ids,
 	.probe = sep_sensor_probe,
@@ -528,26 +619,16 @@ static int sep_sensor_cs_timing_mode_locked(void)
 }
 
 /* Power cycle: off, 10 ms, on, 7 ms. -ENODEV if there is no power line. */
-static int sep_sensor_power_cycle_locked(void)
+static int sep_sensor_power_cycle_locked(struct sep_sensor_lifetime *sensor)
 {
-	if (sep_power_source == SEP_POWER_SMC_RAILS) {
-		int rc = sep_smc_set_power(0);
-
-		if (rc)
-			return rc;
-		msleep(SEP_SENSOR_OFF_DELAY_MS);
-		rc = sep_smc_set_power(1);
-		if (rc)
-			return rc;
-		msleep(SEP_SENSOR_ON_DELAY_MS);
-		return 0;
-	}
-	if (!sep_power)
-		return -ENODEV;
+	int rc = sep_sensor_write_power(sensor, &sep_hw, 0);
 
-	gpiod_set_value_cansleep(sep_power, 0);
+	if (rc)
+		return rc;
 	msleep(SEP_SENSOR_OFF_DELAY_MS);
-	gpiod_set_value_cansleep(sep_power, 1);
+	rc = sep_sensor_write_power(sensor, &sep_hw, 1);
+	if (rc)
+		return rc;
 	msleep(SEP_SENSOR_ON_DELAY_MS);
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -588,24 +669,13 @@ static const char *sep_sensor_firmware_name_locked(unsigned int index)
 }
 
 /* Powers the sensor on/off, holding the hardware settling delay. */
-static int sep_sensor_power_locked(int on)
+static int sep_sensor_power_locked(struct sep_sensor_lifetime *sensor, int on)
 {
-	if (sep_power_source == SEP_POWER_SMC_RAILS) {
-		int rc = sep_smc_set_power(on ? 1 : 0);
+	int rc = sep_sensor_write_power(sensor, &sep_hw, on ? 1 : 0);
 
-		if (rc)
-			return rc;
-		msleep(on ? SEP_SENSOR_ON_DELAY_MS : SEP_SENSOR_OFF_DELAY_MS);
-		return 0;
-	}
-	if (!sep_power)
-		return -ENODEV;
-
-	gpiod_set_value_cansleep(sep_power, on ? 1 : 0);
-	if (on)
-		msleep(SEP_SENSOR_ON_DELAY_MS);
-	else
-		msleep(SEP_SENSOR_OFF_DELAY_MS);
+	if (rc)
+		return rc;
+	msleep(on ? SEP_SENSOR_ON_DELAY_MS : SEP_SENSOR_OFF_DELAY_MS);
 	return 0;
 }
 
@@ -846,7 +916,7 @@ int sep_sensor_power_cycle(void)
 
 	if (!sensor)
 		return -ENODEV;
-	result = sep_sensor_power_cycle_locked();
+	result = sep_sensor_awake_locked(sensor) ?: sep_sensor_power_cycle_locked(sensor);
 	mutex_unlock(&sensor->lock);
 	return result;
 }
@@ -894,7 +964,7 @@ int sep_sensor_power(int on)
 
 	if (!sensor)
 		return -ENODEV;
-	result = sep_sensor_power_locked(on);
+	result = sep_sensor_awake_locked(sensor) ?: sep_sensor_power_locked(sensor, on);
 	mutex_unlock(&sensor->lock);
 	return result;
 }
@@ -962,7 +1032,7 @@ int sep_sensor_xfer(const void *tx, void *rx, size_t len)
 
 	if (!sensor)
 		return -ENODEV;
-	result = sep_sensor_xfer_locked(tx, rx, len);
+	result = sep_sensor_awake_locked(sensor) ?: sep_sensor_xfer_locked(tx, rx, len);
 	mutex_unlock(&sensor->lock);
 	return result;
 }
@@ -974,7 +1044,7 @@ int sep_sensor_xfer_tx(const void *tx, size_t len)
 
 	if (!sensor)
 		return -ENODEV;
-	result = sep_sensor_xfer_tx_locked(tx, len);
+	result = sep_sensor_awake_locked(sensor) ?: sep_sensor_xfer_tx_locked(tx, len);
 	mutex_unlock(&sensor->lock);
 	return result;
 }
@@ -986,7 +1056,13 @@ int sep_sensor_xfer2(const void *tx, size_t tx_len, void *rx, size_t rx_len)
 
 	if (!sensor)
 		return -ENODEV;
-	result = sep_sensor_xfer2_locked(tx, tx_len, rx, rx_len);
+	result = sep_sensor_awake_locked(sensor) ?:
+		 sep_sensor_xfer2_locked(tx, tx_len, rx, rx_len);
 	mutex_unlock(&sensor->lock);
 	return result;
 }
+
+/* Like the module's Rust tests, which need KUnit built in. */
+#if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_APPLE_SEP_KUNIT_TEST) && IS_BUILTIN(CONFIG_KUNIT)
+#include "sensor_shim_test.c"
+#endif
diff --git a/drivers/soc/apple/sensor_shim_test.c b/drivers/soc/apple/sensor_shim_test.c
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..a0374e65bb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/soc/apple/sensor_shim_test.c
@@ -0,0 +1,169 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only OR MIT
+/* KUnit tests for the fingerprint sensor's system sleep. No hardware is touched. */
+
+#include <kunit/test.h>
+#include <kunit/test-bug.h>
+
+struct sep_fake_hw {
+	/* 's' for a send, '0' and '1' for power writes, in order. */
+	char calls[8];
+	unsigned int ncalls;
+	u8 sent[16];
+	size_t sent_len;
+	int send_rc;
+	int power_rc;
+};
+
+static struct sep_fake_hw *sep_fake(void)
+{
+	return kunit_get_current_test()->priv;
+}
+
+static void sep_fake_note(struct sep_fake_hw *f, char call)
+{
+	if (f->ncalls < ARRAY_SIZE(f->calls))
+		f->calls[f->ncalls++] = call;
+}
+
+static int sep_fake_send(const void *tx, size_t len)
+{
+	struct sep_fake_hw *f = sep_fake();
+
+	sep_fake_note(f, 's');
+	f->sent_len = min(len, sizeof(f->sent));
+	memcpy(f->sent, tx, f->sent_len);
+	return f->send_rc;
+}
+
+static int sep_fake_set_power(int on)
+{
+	struct sep_fake_hw *f = sep_fake();
+
+	sep_fake_note(f, on ? '1' : '0');
+	return f->power_rc;
+}
+
+static const struct sep_sensor_hw sep_fake_hw = {
+	.set_power = sep_fake_set_power,
+	.send = sep_fake_send,
+};
+
+static int sep_sensor_test_init(struct kunit *test)
+{
+	test->priv = kunit_kzalloc(test, sizeof(struct sep_fake_hw), GFP_KERNEL);
+	KUNIT_ASSERT_NOT_NULL(test, test->priv);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static void sep_sensor_sleep_sends_the_command_then_cuts_power(struct kunit *test)
+{
+	struct sep_fake_hw *f = test->priv;
+	struct sep_sensor_lifetime sensor = { .powered = true };
+
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, sep_sensor_sleep_locked(&sensor, &sep_fake_hw), 0);
+	KUNIT_ASSERT_EQ(test, f->ncalls, 2U);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_MEMEQ(test, f->calls, "s0", 2);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, f->sent_len, sizeof(sep_sensor_cmd_sleep));
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_MEMEQ(test, f->sent, sep_sensor_cmd_sleep, sizeof(sep_sensor_cmd_sleep));
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_FALSE(test, sensor.powered);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_TRUE(test, sensor.asleep);
+}
+
+static void sep_sensor_sleep_leaves_an_unpowered_sensor_alone(struct kunit *test)
+{
+	struct sep_fake_hw *f = test->priv;
+	struct sep_sensor_lifetime sensor = { .powered = false };
+
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, sep_sensor_sleep_locked(&sensor, &sep_fake_hw), 0);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, f->ncalls, 0U);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_FALSE(test, sensor.powered);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_TRUE(test, sensor.asleep);
+}
+
+static void sep_sensor_sleep_cuts_power_when_the_command_fails(struct kunit *test)
+{
+	struct sep_fake_hw *f = test->priv;
+	struct sep_sensor_lifetime sensor = { .powered = true };
+
+	f->send_rc = -ETIMEDOUT;
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, sep_sensor_sleep_locked(&sensor, &sep_fake_hw), 0);
+	KUNIT_ASSERT_EQ(test, f->ncalls, 2U);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_MEMEQ(test, f->calls, "s0", 2);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_FALSE(test, sensor.powered);
+}
+
+static void sep_sensor_sleep_counts_a_failed_power_off_as_on(struct kunit *test)
+{
+	struct sep_fake_hw *f = test->priv;
+	struct sep_sensor_lifetime sensor = { .powered = true };
+
+	f->power_rc = -EIO;
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, sep_sensor_sleep_locked(&sensor, &sep_fake_hw), -EIO);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_TRUE(test, sensor.powered);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_TRUE(test, sensor.asleep);
+}
+
+static void sep_sensor_requests_wait_for_the_resume(struct kunit *test)
+{
+	struct sep_sensor_lifetime sensor = { .powered = true };
+	struct device *dev = kunit_kzalloc(test, sizeof(*dev), GFP_KERNEL);
+
+	KUNIT_ASSERT_NOT_NULL(test, dev);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, sep_sensor_awake_locked(&sensor), 0);
+	sep_sensor_sleep_locked(&sensor, &sep_fake_hw);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, sep_sensor_awake_locked(&sensor), -ESHUTDOWN);
+
+	dev_set_drvdata(dev, &sensor);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, sep_sensor_resume(dev), 0);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, sep_sensor_awake_locked(&sensor), 0);
+	/* The resume powers nothing: the next bring-up does. */
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_FALSE(test, sensor.powered);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, ((struct sep_fake_hw *)test->priv)->ncalls, 2U);
+}
+
+static void sep_sensor_power_writes_are_tracked(struct kunit *test)
+{
+	struct sep_fake_hw *f = test->priv;
+	struct sep_sensor_lifetime sensor = {};
+
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, sep_sensor_write_power(&sensor, &sep_fake_hw, 1), 0);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_TRUE(test, sensor.powered);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, sep_sensor_write_power(&sensor, &sep_fake_hw, 0), 0);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_FALSE(test, sensor.powered);
+
+	f->power_rc = -EIO;
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, sep_sensor_write_power(&sensor, &sep_fake_hw, 1), -EIO);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_TRUE(test, sensor.powered);
+	sensor.powered = false;
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, sep_sensor_write_power(&sensor, &sep_fake_hw, 0), -EIO);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_TRUE(test, sensor.powered);
+}
+
+/* Framed like the driver's other commands: 0x80, opcode, then the length at 3. */
+static void sep_sensor_sleep_command_is_framed(struct kunit *test)
+{
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, sizeof(sep_sensor_cmd_sleep), (size_t)7);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, sep_sensor_cmd_sleep[0], (u8)0x80);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, sep_sensor_cmd_sleep[1], (u8)0x31);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, (size_t)get_unaligned_le16(&sep_sensor_cmd_sleep[3]),
+			sizeof(sep_sensor_cmd_sleep));
+}
+
+static struct kunit_case sep_sensor_sleep_cases[] = {
+	KUNIT_CASE(sep_sensor_sleep_sends_the_command_then_cuts_power),
+	KUNIT_CASE(sep_sensor_sleep_leaves_an_unpowered_sensor_alone),
+	KUNIT_CASE(sep_sensor_sleep_cuts_power_when_the_command_fails),
+	KUNIT_CASE(sep_sensor_sleep_counts_a_failed_power_off_as_on),
+	KUNIT_CASE(sep_sensor_requests_wait_for_the_resume),
+	KUNIT_CASE(sep_sensor_power_writes_are_tracked),
+	KUNIT_CASE(sep_sensor_sleep_command_is_framed),
+	{}
+};
+
+static struct kunit_suite sep_sensor_sleep_suite = {
+	.name = "apple_sep_sensor_sleep",
+	.init = sep_sensor_test_init,
+	.test_cases = sep_sensor_sleep_cases,
+};
+
+kunit_test_suite(sep_sensor_sleep_suite);
